title: Prototyping
---
<h1 class="govuk-heading-xl">Make a prototype</h1>

<p class="govuk-body-l">
  A step-by-step guide to building a Defra prototype. You do not need to be able
  to code, and you do not need to have used a terminal before.
</p>

<div class="govuk-inset-text">
  A prototype is for showing ideas and testing them with users. It is not a real
  service. Never put real personal data into one.
</div>

<h2 class="govuk-heading-l">Before you start</h2>

<p class="govuk-body">You need two things installed on your computer. Both are free.</p>

<h3 class="govuk-heading-m">1. Node.js</h3>
<p class="govuk-body">
  This runs the prototype on your machine. Go to
  <a class="govuk-link" href="https://nodejs.org">nodejs.org</a> and download the
  version marked <strong>LTS</strong>. Open the file you downloaded and follow the
  instructions.
</p>

<h3 class="govuk-heading-m">2. Git</h3>
<p class="govuk-body">
  This saves versions of your work and lets you share it. On a Mac it is usually
  already installed. On Windows, download it from
  <a class="govuk-link" href="https://git-scm.com/downloads">git-scm.com</a>.
</p>

<h2 class="govuk-heading-l">Open a terminal</h2>

<p class="govuk-body">
  A terminal is a window where you type commands instead of clicking. You will
  copy and paste into it — you do not need to memorise anything.
</p>

<ul class="govuk-list govuk-list--bullet">
  <li><strong>Mac</strong> — press Command and Space, type <code>Terminal</code>, press Enter</li>
  <li><strong>Windows</strong> — press the Start button, type <code>PowerShell</code>, press Enter</li>
</ul>

<p class="govuk-body">
  To check Node.js installed correctly, type this and press Enter:
</p>
<pre class="govuk-body" style="padding:1rem;background:#f3f2f1;border:1px solid #b1b4b6;overflow-x:auto"><code>node --version</code></pre>
<p class="govuk-body">
  You should see a number like <code>v22.20.0</code>. If the number is lower than
  20, or you see an error, install Node.js again from the link above and then
  close and reopen the terminal.
</p>

<h2 class="govuk-heading-l">Choose where to keep your prototypes</h2>

<p class="govuk-body">
  Make a folder to keep prototypes in, so they are not scattered around your
  computer. These commands make a folder called <code>prototypes</code> in your
  home folder and move into it. Run them one at a time.
</p>

<pre class="govuk-body" style="padding:1rem;background:#f3f2f1;border:1px solid #b1b4b6;overflow-x:auto"><code>cd ~
mkdir prototypes
cd prototypes</code></pre>

<dl class="govuk-summary-list govuk-summary-list--no-border govuk-body-s">
  <div class="govuk-summary-list__row">
    <dt class="govuk-summary-list__key"><code>cd ~</code></dt>
    <dd class="govuk-summary-list__value">Go to your home folder. <code>cd</code> means "change directory"</dd>
  </div>
  <div class="govuk-summary-list__row">
    <dt class="govuk-summary-list__key"><code>mkdir prototypes</code></dt>
    <dd class="govuk-summary-list__value">Make a new folder called <code>prototypes</code></dd>
  </div>
  <div class="govuk-summary-list__row">
    <dt class="govuk-summary-list__key"><code>cd prototypes</code></dt>
    <dd class="govuk-summary-list__value">Move into that folder</dd>
  </div>
</dl>

<p class="govuk-body-s">
  You only need to do this once. Next time, just run <code>cd ~/prototypes</code>.
</p>

<h2 class="govuk-heading-l">Create the prototype</h2>

<p class="govuk-body">
  Replace <code>my-service</code> with a short name for what you are prototyping,
  using hyphens instead of spaces — for example <code>water-abstraction</code>.
</p>

<pre class="govuk-body" style="padding:1rem;background:#f3f2f1;border:1px solid #b1b4b6;overflow-x:auto"><code>npx @defra-design/create-prototype my-service</code></pre>

<p class="govuk-body">
  This takes a few minutes. It will print a lot of text — that is normal. If it
  asks <code>Ok to proceed? (y)</code>, type <code>y</code> and press Enter.
</p>

<details class="govuk-details">
  <summary class="govuk-details__summary">
    <span class="govuk-details__summary-text">If that command does not work</span>
  </summary>
  <div class="govuk-details__text">
    <p class="govuk-body">
      You need access to the Defra GitHub organisation for it to fetch the code.
      If it cannot, download the code first and run it from there instead:
    </p>
    <pre class="govuk-body" style="padding:1rem;background:#fff;border:1px solid #b1b4b6;overflow-x:auto"><code>git clone https://github.com/defra-design/defra-frontend.git
node defra-frontend/packages/create-prototype/bin/create.mjs my-service</code></pre>
    <p class="govuk-body">
      Both routes produce exactly the same prototype.
    </p>
  </div>
</details>

<p class="govuk-body">It creates the folder and sets up everything for you:</p>
<ul class="govuk-list govuk-list--bullet govuk-body-s">
  <li>the GOV.UK Prototype Kit</li>
  <li>Defra styling, so your pages look like a Defra service</li>
  <li>Defra page templates</li>
  <li>a README explaining how to run it, so you do not have to remember</li>
  <li>a first saved version, so your work is protected from the start</li>
</ul>

<p class="govuk-body-s">
  Defra Frontend is not on npm yet, so a copy is placed in a <code>vendor</code>
  folder inside your prototype and installed from there. You do not need to do
  anything with it — it is included when you share the prototype, so it works on
  a colleague's machine too.
</p>

<h2 class="govuk-heading-l">Start it</h2>

<pre class="govuk-body" style="padding:1rem;background:#f3f2f1;border:1px solid #b1b4b6;overflow-x:auto"><code>cd my-service
npm start</code></pre>

<p class="govuk-body">
  Open <a class="govuk-link" href="http://localhost:3000">http://localhost:3000</a>
  in your browser. You should see your prototype.
</p>

<p class="govuk-body">
  Leave the terminal open while you work — it is running the prototype. To stop
  it, click the terminal and press <strong>Ctrl</strong> and <strong>C</strong>
  together.
</p>

<h2 class="govuk-heading-l">Make your first page</h2>

<p class="govuk-body">
  Go to
  <a class="govuk-link" href="http://localhost:3000/manage-prototype">http://localhost:3000/manage-prototype</a>
  and choose <strong>Create a new page</strong>. Pick one of the Defra templates:
</p>

<ul class="govuk-list govuk-list--bullet">
  <li><strong>Defra start page</strong> — the first page of a service</li>
  <li><strong>Defra question page</strong> — one question, as the service pattern requires</li>
  <li><strong>Defra check answers page</strong> — the summary before submitting</li>
</ul>

<p class="govuk-body">
  Pages live in the <code>app/views</code> folder and are ordinary HTML. A file at
  <code>app/views/apply/name.html</code> appears at <code>/apply/name</code>.
  Open them in any text editor —
  <a class="govuk-link" href="https://code.visualstudio.com/">Visual Studio Code</a>
  is free and widely used.
</p>

<h2 class="govuk-heading-l">Putting your prototype on GitHub</h2>

<p class="govuk-body">
  Put your prototype on GitHub so it is backed up, so colleagues can see it, and
  so it can be handed over. Your prototype is already saving versions locally —
  this puts those versions somewhere safe.
</p>

<h3 class="govuk-heading-m">1. Create an empty repository</h3>
<p class="govuk-body">
  Go to <a class="govuk-link" href="https://github.com/new">github.com/new</a> and:
</p>
<ul class="govuk-list govuk-list--bullet">
  <li>choose the Defra organisation as the owner, if you can</li>
  <li>name it the same as your folder</li>
  <li>leave it <strong>private</strong> unless your team has agreed otherwise</li>
  <li>do <strong>not</strong> tick "Add a README" — you already have one</li>
</ul>

<h3 class="govuk-heading-m">2. Connect and upload</h3>
<p class="govuk-body">
  GitHub shows you a page of commands. Use the two under
  <strong>"…or push an existing repository"</strong>. They look like this, with
  your own names in place of <code>DEFRA</code> and <code>my-service</code>:
</p>
<pre class="govuk-body" style="padding:1rem;background:#f3f2f1;border:1px solid #b1b4b6;overflow-x:auto"><code>git remote add origin https://github.com/DEFRA/my-service.git
git push -u origin main</code></pre>

<h3 class="govuk-heading-m">3. Save your work as you go</h3>
<p class="govuk-body">
  Each time you finish something worth keeping, run these three lines. The
  message should say what you changed.
</p>
<pre class="govuk-body" style="padding:1rem;background:#f3f2f1;border:1px solid #b1b4b6;overflow-x:auto"><code>git add -A
git commit -m "Add the eligibility questions"
git push</code></pre>

<div class="govuk-inset-text">
  Before pushing, check you have not written anything real into your pages —
  names, addresses, case references. A private repository is not a safe place for
  personal data, and once something is pushed it stays in the history even if you
  delete it later.
</div>

<h2 class="govuk-heading-l">Sharing with users for research</h2>

<p class="govuk-body">
  <code>localhost</code> only works on your own machine, so you cannot send that
  address to anyone. To test with users remotely the prototype needs hosting
  somewhere, which needs a password on it.
</p>
<p class="govuk-body">
  Hosting is a team decision and depends on what your programme already uses, so
  ask your technical lead rather than setting one up alone. The Prototype Kit
  documentation covers
  <a class="govuk-link" href="https://prototype-kit.service.gov.uk/docs/publishing-on-the-web">the options and how to password-protect them</a>.
</p>

<h2 class="govuk-heading-l">Updating an existing prototype</h2>

<p class="govuk-body">
  Your prototype stays on the version of Defra Frontend it was made with, so
  fixes and new components do not reach it on their own. To bring it up to
  date, run this from inside your prototype folder:
</p>

<pre class="govuk-body" style="padding:1rem;background:#f3f2f1;border:1px solid #b1b4b6;overflow-x:auto"><code>npx @defra-design/create-prototype --update</code></pre>

<p class="govuk-body">
  It reports the version before and after, and tells you if your prototype
  contains workarounds that are no longer needed. Restart with
  <code>npm start</code> afterwards.
</p>

<p class="govuk-body-s">
  Nothing else in your prototype is touched — your pages, your layout and your
  data stay exactly as they are.
</p>

<details class="govuk-details">
  <summary class="govuk-details__summary">
    <span class="govuk-details__summary-text">If you made your prototype before October 2026</span>
  </summary>
  <div class="govuk-details__text">
    <p class="govuk-body">
      Early prototypes kept their own copy of Defra Frontend in a
      <code>vendor</code> folder, because the package had not been published
      yet. The update command moves yours across to the published package and
      deletes that folder.
    </p>
    <p class="govuk-body">
      You will see a short list of what it changed. Commit those changes along
      with your own work. Nothing you have built is affected.
    </p>
  </div>
</details>

<h2 class="govuk-heading-l">Publishing your prototype</h2>

<p class="govuk-body">
  A prototype is a small web application, not a set of files, so it needs
  somewhere that can run it. That rules out the simplest options and is worth
  knowing before you promise a link to anyone.
</p>

<dl class="govuk-summary-list govuk-body-s">
  <div class="govuk-summary-list__row">
    <dt class="govuk-summary-list__key">A hosting service</dt>
    <dd class="govuk-summary-list__value">
      The usual route, and the only one that supports every prototype. Your
      programme almost certainly already uses something — ask your technical
      lead rather than signing up for an account yourself. The kit documents
      <a class="govuk-link" href="https://prototype-kit.service.gov.uk/docs/publishing-on-the-web">the options and how to password-protect them</a>.
    </dd>
  </div>
  <div class="govuk-summary-list__row">
    <dt class="govuk-summary-list__key">GitHub Pages</dt>
    <dd class="govuk-summary-list__value">
      Possible, but only for a prototype that is pages and links. Pages serves
      files and cannot run anything, so you first have to export your prototype
      to flat HTML. Anything that remembers an answer between pages — a form
      post, branching based on what someone entered, the <code>data</code>
      object — stops working. If your prototype only links from page to page,
      this is the cheapest option; if it collects answers, it is not an option
      at all.
    </dd>
  </div>
</dl>

<div class="govuk-inset-text">
  Whatever you choose, put a password on it. A prototype looks like a real
  service, and people who find one without context have no way of knowing it
  is not. Never put real personal data in a published prototype.
</div>

<h2 class="govuk-heading-l">If something goes wrong</h2>

<dl class="govuk-summary-list govuk-body-s">
  <div class="govuk-summary-list__row">
    <dt class="govuk-summary-list__key"><code>command not found: npx</code></dt>
    <dd class="govuk-summary-list__value">Node.js is not installed, or the terminal was open before you installed it. Close the terminal, open a new one, and try again.</dd>
  </div>
  <div class="govuk-summary-list__row">
    <dt class="govuk-summary-list__key"><code>EADDRINUSE</code> or "port already in use"</dt>
    <dd class="govuk-summary-list__value">A prototype is already running in another terminal window. Find it and press Ctrl and C, or close that window.</dd>
  </div>
  <div class="govuk-summary-list__row">
    <dt class="govuk-summary-list__key">The page looks unstyled</dt>
    <dd class="govuk-summary-list__value">Stop the prototype with Ctrl and C, then run <code>npm start</code> again. The styles are rebuilt on start.</dd>
  </div>
  <div class="govuk-summary-list__row">
    <dt class="govuk-summary-list__key">A change does not appear</dt>
    <dd class="govuk-summary-list__value">Save the file, then refresh the browser. If it still does not appear, check you edited a file inside <code>app/views</code>.</dd>
  </div>
</dl>

<h2 class="govuk-heading-l">Which layout to use</h2>

<p class="govuk-body">
  Your prototype uses the Defra layout: a Defra header and footer, and system
  fonts rather than the GOV.UK typeface. That is not a style preference — GDS
  Transport and the GOV.UK header and footer are licensed for services on
  <code>*.service.gov.uk</code> only.
</p>
<p class="govuk-body">
  If your service <em>will</em> be on <code>*.service.gov.uk</code>, change the
  first line of <code>app/views/layouts/main.html</code> to extend
  <code>"govuk-prototype-kit/layouts/govuk-branded.njk"</code> instead. If you are
  not sure which applies, ask before you show the prototype to anyone outside the
  team.
</p>
